%ADVECTION.M%advection of substance, s. hsiang %(smh2137@columbia.edu), aug, 2007%how many time steps we want to integrate forward (you can change this %later if you're curious):timelimit = 30;%initial conditions for the integration are set to our initial %concentration distribution c that defined manuallyct = c;%flow speed in the positive direction (this can be changed later if you're %curious):u=1;%here we integrate the model by taking the initial conditions, use them to %calculate how the model will change in the next time step and adding that %change to the old conditionsfor i = 1:timelimit;dcdx = diff( [0, ct(i,:)] );%diff creates a vector that is one shorter than its argument where each %element is the difference between the elements of its argument. type %"help diff" to get a more complete description.%we include the zero in dcdx before the diff vector because the diff %vector is one shorter than our ct rows. it's zero because we assume %that the grid box just off the left side of the model has %concentration equal to zero, so that nothing is getting advected into %the model from the boundary.%then we use the equation that we just learned: %Dc/Dt = dc/dt - u * grad(c)%except here we have dc/ct = 0.DcDt = 0 - u * dcdx;%we find the new concentration distribution by adding Dc/Dt to our old %concentration.newc = ct(i,:) + DcDt;%Then we record the new distribution by adding it as another row to our %'record matrix'. ct will end up being a matrix where each row is the %distibution of the substance at the end of a certain time periods, %with the time periods corrosponding to the row number minus onect = [ct; newc];end%our result is the record matrix ct. we look at ct to see what happened %during out simulation.